<title>USA: Florida's citrus sees 'ideal' conditions - FASS.</title>
<headline>Florida's citrus sees 'ideal' conditions - FASS.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-07-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Florida's citrus belt had more high temperatures, heavy rains and thunderstorms this week, providing nearly ideal growing conditions, according to Florida Agricultural Statistics Service (FASS).</p>
<p>In its weekly Weather &amp; Crop News report, FASS reported adequate moisture in all areas of the belt, with new foliage and cover crops growing in abundance.</p>
<p>Fruit quality is variable, with groves that have discontinued care showing signs of scab, melanose and various insects, FASS said.</p>
<p>Caretakers in the region are very active cutting cover crops prior to fertilizations. Summer spraying is being slowed by frequent rains. Dead tree removal and brush burning continue in all areas, according to FASS.</p>
<p>-- New York Commodity Desk, 212-859-1640</p>
